var e=[{id:"message-queue-basics",title:"Message Queue Fundamentals",category:"fundamentals",order:1,explanation:`A message queue is an asynchronous communication mechanism that decouples producers (senders) from consumers (receivers). Instead of calling a service directly and waiting for a response, the producer drops a message onto a queue and continues its work. A consumer picks up the message later, processes it, and optionally acknowledges completion. This pattern is the backbone of any distributed system that needs to handle spikes, tolerate failures, or scale components independently.

Two primary messaging models exist. Point-to-point (queue) delivers each message to exactly one consumer -- ideal for task distribution like order processing where you do not want duplicate work. Publish/subscribe (topic) delivers each message to every subscriber -- ideal for event broadcasting like notifying search, analytics, and notification services when a user signs up.

Delivery semantics are a critical design decision. At-most-once means the broker delivers the message once and never retries, risking data loss but offering the lowest latency. At-least-once means the broker retries until acknowledged, potentially causing duplicates that the consumer must handle idempotently. Exactly-once is the gold standard: the broker and consumer cooperate (via transactions or idempotent writes) to ensure each message is processed exactly once. Kafka achieves this through idempotent producers and transactional consumers, though at a performance cost.`,useCases:[{title:"Order Processing Pipeline",scenario:"An e-commerce checkout must validate payment, reserve inventory, send confirmation email, and update analytics. Doing all of this synchronously in the HTTP request makes the checkout slow and fragile -- if the email service is down, the order fails.",implementation:"Publish an OrderPlaced event to a message queue after payment succeeds. Independent consumers handle inventory reservation, email sending, and analytics ingestion in parallel. Each consumer can retry independently without blocking others. Use a dead letter queue for messages that fail after max retries."},{title:"Rate Limiting External API Calls",scenario:"Your service needs to call a third-party API that enforces a rate limit of 100 requests per minute. During peak traffic, your app generates 1000 requests per minute, causing 429 errors and dropped data.",implementation:"Produce all API call requests to a queue. A single consumer pulls messages at a controlled rate (100/min), calls the external API, and writes results back. The queue acts as a buffer, smoothing out traffic spikes. Combine with exponential backoff on 429 responses."}],keyCommands:[{command:"docker run -d --name rabbitmq -p 5672:5672 -p 15672:15672 rabbitmq:3-management",description:"Start RabbitMQ with management UI for quick point-to-point queue experimentation"},{command:"docker run -d --name redis -p 6379:6379 redis:7-alpine",description:"Start Redis for lightweight pub/sub or stream-based messaging"},{command:"aws sqs create-queue --queue-name orders-queue",description:"Create an SQS queue for managed, serverless message queuing on AWS"},{command:`aws sqs send-message --queue-url <url> --message-body '{"orderId": 123}'`,description:"Send a test message to an SQS queue"}],bestPractices:["Design consumers to be idempotent: processing the same message twice should produce the same result","Always configure a dead letter queue (DLQ) to capture messages that fail after max retries","Set appropriate visibility timeouts or acknowledgment windows to prevent message loss during consumer crashes","Monitor queue depth as a key operational metric -- growing queues indicate consumers cannot keep up","Use message schemas (JSON Schema, Protobuf, Avro) and version them to prevent breaking changes"],interviewQuestions:["What is the difference between a message queue and a message broker?","Explain the trade-offs between at-most-once, at-least-once, and exactly-once delivery semantics.","How would you design an idempotent consumer? Give a concrete example.","When would you choose point-to-point over pub/sub messaging?","What happens to in-flight messages when a consumer crashes? How do different brokers handle this?"],resources:[{label:"Martin Kleppmann: Designing Data-Intensive Applications (Ch. 11)",url:"https://dataintensive.net/"},{label:"AWS: What is a Message Queue?",url:"https://aws.amazon.com/message-queue/"}]},{id:"event-driven-architecture",title:"Event-Driven Architecture",category:"fundamentals",order:2,explanation:`Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) is a design paradigm where system components communicate by producing and reacting to events rather than making direct synchronous calls. An event represents a fact that has occurred -- "OrderPlaced", "PaymentReceived", "InventoryUpdated" -- and is immutable once published. This decoupling means producers do not need to know who consumes their events, enabling independent scaling, deployment, and evolution of services.

Event sourcing takes this further by storing the full sequence of events as the system of record rather than just the current state. Instead of a mutable "orders" table, you append OrderCreated, ItemAdded, OrderPaid events to an event store. The current state is derived by replaying events. This gives you a complete audit trail, the ability to rebuild state at any point in time, and easy debugging ("what happened to order #1234?"). CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) often accompanies event sourcing: writes go through a command model that produces events, while reads are served by denormalized projections optimized for query patterns.

Two coordination styles exist for multi-service workflows. Choreography is peer-to-peer: each service reacts to events and publishes its own events, forming an implicit workflow (OrderService publishes OrderPlaced, InventoryService reacts and publishes InventoryReserved, PaymentService reacts next). Orchestration uses a central coordinator (saga orchestrator) that explicitly tells each service what to do and handles failures. Choreography is simpler but harder to monitor; orchestration adds a single point of coordination but makes the workflow explicit and traceable.`,useCases:[{title:"Real-Time Analytics Pipeline",scenario:"A SaaS platform needs to track user behavior (page views, clicks, feature usage) for product analytics, billing, and personalization. Instrumenting each analytics consumer directly in the application creates tight coupling and performance overhead.",implementation:"Emit UserAction events from the frontend and backend to a Kafka topic. Multiple consumers process these events independently: one writes to ClickHouse for real-time dashboards, another aggregates for billing, a third feeds the recommendation engine. Adding a new consumer requires zero changes to the producer."},{title:"Saga Pattern for Distributed Transactions",scenario:"A travel booking system must reserve a flight, hotel, and car rental atomically. If hotel reservation fails after flight is booked, the flight must be cancelled. A distributed transaction (2PC) is too slow and fragile across multiple third-party APIs.",implementation:"Use the saga pattern with compensating transactions. The booking orchestrator publishes BookFlight, then waits for FlightBooked. On success, publishes BookHotel. If BookHotel fails, publishes CancelFlight as a compensating action. Each step is an event on Kafka, with the orchestrator tracking saga state in a database."},{title:"Audit Log via Event Sourcing",scenario:"A financial system requires a tamper-proof audit trail of every change to accounts, transactions, and user permissions for regulatory compliance.",implementation:"Store all mutations as immutable events in an append-only event store (Kafka with infinite retention or EventStoreDB). The current account balance is a projection derived by replaying AccountCredited and AccountDebited events. Auditors can reconstruct the exact state at any point in time by replaying events up to that timestamp."}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-topics.sh --create --topic user-events --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3 --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Create a partitioned topic for user events with replication"},{command:"kafka-console-producer.sh --topic user-events --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Produce test events to the user-events topic interactively"},{command:"kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic user-events --from-beginning --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Consume all events from the beginning of the topic"}],bestPractices:["Use past-tense verb names for events (OrderPlaced, not PlaceOrder) to reflect that events are immutable facts","Include a correlation ID in every event to trace a business transaction across multiple services","Design events to be self-contained: include enough data so consumers do not need to call back to the producer","Version your event schemas from day one; use a schema registry (Confluent, AWS Glue) to enforce compatibility","Prefer choreography for simple flows (2-3 services) and orchestration for complex flows (4+ services with branching logic)","Implement idempotency at the consumer level using event IDs or natural business keys"],interviewQuestions:["What is the difference between event sourcing and traditional CRUD? When would you choose one over the other?","Explain CQRS. Why is it often paired with event sourcing?","Compare choreography vs orchestration for saga patterns. What are the trade-offs?","How do you handle schema evolution in an event-driven system?","What challenges arise from eventual consistency in event-driven architectures, and how do you mitigate them?"],resources:[{label:"Martin Fowler: Event Sourcing",url:"https://martinfowler.com/eaaDev/EventSourcing.html"},{label:"Microservices.io: Saga Pattern",url:"https://microservices.io/patterns/data/saga.html"}]},{id:"kafka-architecture",title:"Kafka Architecture",category:"kafka",order:3,explanation:`Apache Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform designed for high-throughput, fault-tolerant, real-time data pipelines. At its core, Kafka is a distributed commit log: producers append records to the end of a topic partition, and consumers read sequentially from any offset. This append-only, sequential I/O design is why Kafka achieves millions of messages per second on commodity hardware.

A Kafka cluster consists of brokers (servers) that store partitions. A topic is divided into partitions, each an ordered, immutable sequence of records. Partitions are the unit of parallelism: more partitions mean more consumers can read in parallel. Each partition is replicated across multiple brokers for fault tolerance. One replica is the leader (handles reads/writes), and the others are followers (replicate data). If the leader fails, a follower is elected as the new leader. The in-sync replica set (ISR) tracks which followers are caught up.

Historically, Apache ZooKeeper managed broker metadata, leader election, and topic configuration. KRaft (Kafka Raft) replaces ZooKeeper by embedding the metadata management directly into Kafka brokers using a Raft-based consensus protocol. KRaft simplifies operations (one fewer system to manage), improves startup time, and removes the ZooKeeper scalability bottleneck. As of Kafka 3.5+, KRaft is production-ready and recommended for new deployments.`,useCases:[{title:"Microservice Event Bus",scenario:"A platform with 50+ microservices needs a reliable, high-throughput event bus. Services publish domain events and subscribe to events from other services. The bus must handle 100K events/second with sub-second latency.",implementation:"Deploy a multi-broker Kafka cluster with KRaft mode. Create topics per domain (orders, payments, inventory) with partitions based on expected throughput (start with 6, scale to 12+). Use consumer groups so each microservice processes each event exactly once. Set replication factor to 3 for fault tolerance."},{title:"Change Data Capture (CDC)",scenario:"You need to replicate database changes to a data warehouse in near real-time for analytics, without impacting the production database with direct queries.",implementation:"Use Debezium (Kafka Connect source connector) to capture row-level changes from MySQL/PostgreSQL binlog. Changes are published as events to Kafka topics (one per table). A sink connector writes to the data warehouse (Snowflake, BigQuery). The Kafka topic serves as a durable buffer and replay mechanism."}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-metadata.sh --snapshot /var/kafka-logs/__cluster_metadata-0/00000000000000000000.log --cluster-id <id>",description:"Inspect KRaft metadata log (replaces ZooKeeper shell commands)"},{command:"kafka-broker-api-versions.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"List supported API versions for each broker to check compatibility"},{command:"kafka-topics.sh --describe --topic orders --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Show partition layout, replication factor, ISR, and leader for a topic"},{command:"kafka-log-dirs.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --describe",description:"Show disk usage per topic-partition on each broker"},{command:"kafka-reassign-partitions.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --reassignment-json-file plan.json --execute",description:"Rebalance partitions across brokers after adding new nodes"}],bestPractices:["Use KRaft mode for new Kafka clusters (3.5+) to eliminate the ZooKeeper dependency","Set replication factor to at least 3 and min.insync.replicas to 2 for production topics","Size partitions based on target throughput: each partition can handle ~10 MB/s writes on typical hardware","Keep partition count reasonable (< 200K across the cluster) to avoid excessive metadata overhead","Store Kafka data on dedicated SSD volumes separate from the OS disk","Use rack-aware replication (broker.rack config) to survive availability zone failures"],interviewQuestions:["How does Kafka achieve high throughput? Explain the role of sequential I/O, zero-copy, and batching.","What is the difference between a partition leader and follower? What happens during leader election?","Explain the ISR (In-Sync Replica) set. What does it mean when a replica falls out of ISR?","How does KRaft differ from ZooKeeper-based Kafka? What problems does it solve?","How would you decide the number of partitions for a new topic?"],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Kafka Architecture",url:"https://developer.confluent.io/courses/architecture/get-started/"},{label:"Apache Kafka Documentation",url:"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/"}]},{id:"kafka-producers",title:"Kafka Producers",category:"kafka",order:4,explanation:`A Kafka producer is a client that publishes records to Kafka topics. The producer is responsible for choosing which partition within a topic to send each record to. By default, records with a key are hashed (murmur2) to determine the partition, ensuring all records with the same key go to the same partition and maintain ordering. Records without a key are distributed round-robin across partitions for load balancing.

The acks configuration controls durability guarantees. acks=0 means fire-and-forget (highest throughput, risk of data loss). acks=1 means the leader acknowledges before the followers replicate (balanced). acks=all (or -1) means all in-sync replicas must acknowledge, providing the strongest durability guarantee. Combined with min.insync.replicas=2 and replication.factor=3, acks=all ensures no data loss even if one broker fails.

Idempotent producers (enable.idempotence=true, default since Kafka 3.0) prevent duplicate messages caused by producer retries. The producer assigns a sequence number to each message, and the broker deduplicates based on producer ID + sequence number. Batching (batch.size and linger.ms) groups multiple records into a single network request, dramatically improving throughput. Compression (compression.type=snappy or lz4) reduces network bandwidth and disk usage at a small CPU cost.`,useCases:[{title:"High-Throughput Log Ingestion",scenario:"An application generates 500K log lines per second from 200 servers. Each log must be reliably ingested into Kafka for downstream processing by ELK stack and anomaly detection.",implementation:"Configure producers with batch.size=64KB, linger.ms=5 (wait up to 5ms to fill batches), compression.type=lz4, and acks=1 (acceptable for logs). Use the server hostname as the message key to keep logs from the same server in the same partition for ordered processing. Deploy producers as sidecar containers or use Filebeat with Kafka output."},{title:"Financial Transaction Publishing",scenario:"A payment service publishes transaction events that must never be lost or duplicated. Duplicate processing could charge a customer twice.",implementation:"Enable idempotent producer (enable.idempotence=true) and set acks=all with min.insync.replicas=2. Use the transaction ID as the message key to ensure all events for a transaction go to the same partition. Enable Kafka transactions (transactional.id) if the producer writes to multiple topics atomically. Monitor producer metrics: record-send-rate, record-error-rate, and request-latency-avg."}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-console-producer.sh --topic payments --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --property parse.key=true --property key.separator=:",description:"Produce keyed messages (key:value format) to test partition routing"},{command:"kafka-producer-perf-test.sh --topic perf-test --num-records 1000000 --record-size 1024 --throughput -1 --producer-props bootstrap.servers=localhost:9092",description:"Run a producer performance benchmark (1M records, 1KB each, max throughput)"},{command:"kafka-console-producer.sh --topic orders --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --producer-property acks=all --producer-property compression.type=snappy",description:"Produce with acks=all and snappy compression for durability testing"},{command:"kafka-run-class.sh kafka.tools.GetOffsetShell --broker-list localhost:9092 --topic payments --time -1",description:"Get the latest offset (end) for each partition to verify messages were produced"}],bestPractices:["Always set a message key when ordering matters: all records with the same key go to the same partition","Enable idempotent producers in production to prevent duplicates from network retries","Use acks=all for critical data (payments, orders) and acks=1 for high-volume, loss-tolerant data (logs, metrics)","Tune batch.size (16-64KB) and linger.ms (5-20ms) based on your latency vs throughput trade-off","Use lz4 or snappy compression for best throughput; zstd for best compression ratio"],interviewQuestions:["What happens when a Kafka producer sends a message with acks=all and one of the ISR replicas is down?","How does the default partitioner decide which partition to send a record to?","What is an idempotent producer and how does it prevent duplicates?","Explain the trade-off between batch.size, linger.ms, and end-to-end latency.","How would you handle a producer that gets a NotLeaderForPartitionException?"],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Kafka Producer Configuration",url:"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/installation/configuration/producer-configs.html"},{label:"Kafka Producer Javadoc",url:"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#producerapi"}]},{id:"kafka-consumers",title:"Kafka Consumers",category:"kafka",order:5,explanation:`Kafka consumers read records from topics by tracking their position (offset) in each partition. A consumer group is a set of consumers that cooperate to consume a topic: each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in the group, enabling parallel processing. If you have 6 partitions and 3 consumers in a group, each consumer reads from 2 partitions. Adding a 4th consumer triggers a rebalance that redistributes partitions. Having more consumers than partitions means some consumers sit idle.

Offset management determines when a message is considered "processed." Auto-commit (enable.auto.commit=true) periodically commits offsets in the background, which is simple but risks reprocessing (consumer crashes after processing but before commit) or data loss (commit happens before processing completes). Manual commit gives precise control: commit after successful processing to guarantee at-least-once delivery. For exactly-once, combine consumer-side offset commits with transactional writes to the output store.

Rebalancing occurs when consumers join/leave the group or partitions are added. During a rebalance, all consumers in the group stop processing, which can cause latency spikes. Cooperative rebalancing (CooperativeStickyAssignor) minimizes disruption by only reassigning affected partitions instead of revoking all partitions. Static group membership (group.instance.id) reduces rebalances from transient consumer restarts by telling Kafka to wait (session.timeout.ms) before assuming the consumer is dead.`,useCases:[{title:"Competing Consumers for Order Processing",scenario:"An order processing pipeline receives 10K orders per minute. A single consumer cannot keep up. You need horizontal scaling without duplicate processing.",implementation:'Create a consumer group "order-processors" with 6 consumers (matching the 6 partitions). Each consumer processes orders from its assigned partitions. Use the order ID as the message key to ensure all events for the same order go to the same partition and are processed by the same consumer in order. Use manual offset commit after successfully persisting the order to the database.'},{title:"Multi-Team Event Consumption",scenario:"Three teams (billing, analytics, notifications) all need to process the same UserSignup events independently. Each team processes events at different speeds and should not block each other.",implementation:'Each team creates its own consumer group: "billing-consumers", "analytics-consumers", "notifications-consumers". Kafka delivers every message to every consumer group independently. Each group tracks its own offsets. If the analytics consumer falls behind, billing and notifications are unaffected. Monitor consumer lag per group.'}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic orders --group order-processors --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Consume from the orders topic as part of the order-processors consumer group"},{command:"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group order-processors --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Show partition assignments, current offset, log-end offset, and lag per partition"},{command:"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --reset-offsets --group order-processors --topic orders --to-earliest --execute --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Reset consumer group offsets to reprocess all messages from the beginning"},{command:"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --list --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"List all consumer groups in the cluster"},{command:"kafka-consumer-perf-test.sh --topic orders --group perf-group --messages 1000000 --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Benchmark consumer throughput over 1M messages"}],bestPractices:["Match the number of consumers in a group to the number of partitions for maximum parallelism","Use manual offset commit (commitSync/commitAsync) for critical workloads to prevent data loss","Configure CooperativeStickyAssignor to minimize rebalance disruption in production","Set session.timeout.ms and heartbeat.interval.ms appropriately: heartbeat = 1/3 of session timeout","Monitor consumer lag continuously using kafka-consumer-groups.sh or Burrow/Kafka UI","Use static group membership (group.instance.id) for Kubernetes deployments to reduce rebalances during rolling restarts"],interviewQuestions:["What happens when the number of consumers in a group exceeds the number of partitions?","Explain the difference between auto-commit and manual offset commit. When would you use each?","What triggers a consumer group rebalance? How does cooperative rebalancing improve on eager rebalancing?","How do consumer groups enable both competing consumers and pub/sub patterns?","A consumer group has increasing lag. Walk through your debugging process."],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Kafka Consumer Configuration",url:"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/installation/configuration/consumer-configs.html"},{label:"Kafka Consumer Group Protocol (KIP-848)",url:"https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-848"}]},{id:"kafka-connect-streams",title:"Kafka Connect & Streams",category:"kafka",order:6,explanation:`Kafka Connect is a framework for streaming data between Kafka and external systems without writing code. Source connectors pull data into Kafka (databases, files, APIs), and sink connectors push data from Kafka to external systems (data warehouses, search indexes, S3). Connectors run as tasks distributed across a Connect cluster, providing scalability and fault tolerance. The most popular connector is Debezium for change data capture (CDC) from databases.

Kafka Streams is a client library for building stream processing applications directly on top of Kafka. Unlike Spark Streaming or Flink, Kafka Streams does not require a separate cluster -- it runs as a regular Java/Go application. It provides a DSL for common operations: filter, map, groupBy, aggregate, join, and windowed computations. Under the hood, it uses Kafka consumer groups for parallelism and local state stores (RocksDB) for stateful operations.

KTables represent a changelog stream where each record is an upsert keyed by the record key. As new records arrive, they replace the previous value for that key, creating a materialized view of the latest state. Windowed operations (tumbling, hopping, sliding, session windows) enable time-based aggregations like "count events per user per 5-minute window." Joins between KStreams and KTables enable enrichment patterns: join a stream of orders with a table of customer profiles to produce enriched order events.`,useCases:[{title:"Database to Data Lake Pipeline",scenario:"Your MySQL production database needs to be replicated to S3/data lake for analytics without impacting production performance or requiring custom ETL scripts.",implementation:"Deploy Debezium MySQL source connector to capture binlog changes into Kafka topics (one per table). Deploy S3 sink connector to write Parquet files partitioned by date to S3. The pipeline is fully managed by Kafka Connect -- no custom code. Schema evolution is handled by the Confluent Schema Registry with Avro serialization."},{title:"Real-Time Fraud Detection",scenario:"A payment platform needs to detect suspicious patterns in real-time: more than 5 transactions from the same card in 1 minute, or transactions from geographically distant locations within minutes.",implementation:"Use Kafka Streams with a session window keyed by card number. Count transactions within a 1-minute tumbling window. If the count exceeds the threshold, produce an alert event to a fraud-alerts topic. Join the transaction stream with a KTable of known-fraud patterns for rule-based detection. State is stored locally in RocksDB and backed up to Kafka changelog topics for fault tolerance."}],keyCommands:[{command:'curl -X POST http://localhost:8083/connectors -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d @mysql-source.json',description:"Deploy a Kafka Connect connector by POSTing its configuration JSON"},{command:"curl http://localhost:8083/connectors/mysql-source/status",description:"Check the status of a running connector and its tasks"},{command:"curl http://localhost:8083/connector-plugins",description:"List all available connector plugins installed on the Connect cluster"},{command:"curl -X DELETE http://localhost:8083/connectors/mysql-source",description:"Delete a connector and stop all its tasks"},{command:"kafka-streams-application-reset.sh --application-id my-streams-app --input-topics input-topic --bootstrap-servers localhost:9092",description:"Reset a Kafka Streams application to reprocess from the beginning"}],bestPractices:["Use Kafka Connect for standard integrations (databases, S3, Elasticsearch) instead of writing custom producers/consumers","Run Connect in distributed mode with multiple workers for production fault tolerance","Use Avro or Protobuf with Schema Registry for Connect to handle schema evolution automatically","For Kafka Streams, design your topology to minimize state store size by using windowed operations with retention policies","Monitor Connect connector lag and task failures; set up alerts on connector status changes"],interviewQuestions:["What is the difference between Kafka Connect and writing a custom Kafka producer/consumer?","Explain how Debezium captures database changes without impacting production performance.","What is the difference between a KStream and a KTable in Kafka Streams?","How does Kafka Streams handle state? What happens when a Streams application instance fails?","Describe a windowed aggregation use case and how you would implement it with Kafka Streams."],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Kafka Connect",url:"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/connect/index.html"},{label:"Kafka Streams Documentation",url:"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/streams/"}]},{id:"kafka-setup-hands-on",title:"Kafka Hands-On Setup",category:"kafka",order:7,explanation:`Getting Kafka running locally is essential for hands-on learning and development. Docker Compose is the fastest way to spin up a complete Kafka environment with a broker, Schema Registry, Kafka Connect, and a web UI for monitoring. The KRaft mode (no ZooKeeper) simplifies the setup to a single container for the broker.

A practical local setup includes: a Kafka broker (Confluent or Apache image), kafka-ui or Redpanda Console for visual topic/consumer management, and optionally Schema Registry for Avro/Protobuf schemas. With this environment, you can create topics, produce and consume messages, observe consumer groups, and experiment with partitioning and replication without cloud costs.

For development workflows, define your topics as infrastructure-as-code in the Docker Compose file or a setup script. Use the Kafka CLI tools (kafka-topics.sh, kafka-console-producer.sh, kafka-console-consumer.sh) for quick debugging, and the kafka-ui web interface for monitoring consumer lag, browsing messages, and managing topics. When testing, use Testcontainers to spin up an ephemeral Kafka broker per test suite.`,useCases:[{title:"Local Development Environment",scenario:"A development team needs each engineer to run Kafka locally for integration testing. The setup must be reproducible, start in under 30 seconds, and match production configuration as closely as possible.",implementation:`version: "3.8"
services:
  kafka:
    image: confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.6.0
    ports:
      - "9092:9092"
    environment:
      KAFKA_NODE_ID: 1
      KAFKA_PROCESS_ROLES: broker,controller
      KAFKA_CONTROLLER_QUORUM_VOTERS: 1@kafka:29093
      KAFKA_LISTENERS: PLAINTEXT://0.0.0.0:9092,CONTROLLER://0.0.0.0:29093
      KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS: PLAINTEXT://localhost:9092
      KAFKA_CONTROLLER_LISTENER_NAMES: CONTROLLER
      KAFKA_LISTENER_SECURITY_PROTOCOL_MAP: CONTROLLER:PLAINTEXT,PLAINTEXT:PLAINTEXT
      KAFKA_LOG_DIRS: /tmp/kraft-combined-logs
      CLUSTER_ID: MkU3OEVBNTcwNTJENDM2Qk
  kafka-ui:
    image: provectuslabs/kafka-ui:latest
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    environment:
      KAFKA_CLUSTERS_0_NAME: local
      KAFKA_CLUSTERS_0_BOOTSTRAPSERVERS: kafka:9092`}],keyCommands:[{command:"docker compose up -d",description:"Start the Kafka stack (broker + UI) in detached mode"},{command:"kafka-topics.sh --create --topic test-topic --partitions 3 --replication-factor 1 --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Create a test topic with 3 partitions"},{command:"kafka-topics.sh --list --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"List all topics in the cluster"},{command:'echo "hello kafka" | kafka-console-producer.sh --topic test-topic --bootstrap-server localhost:9092',description:"Produce a test message from the command line"},{command:"kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic test-topic --from-beginning --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Consume all messages from a topic starting from the beginning"}],bestPractices:["Use KRaft mode (single-process) for local development to avoid running ZooKeeper","Include kafka-ui or Redpanda Console in your Docker Compose for visual monitoring","Pin Kafka image versions in Docker Compose to match your production Kafka version","Create a setup script that idempotently creates topics with correct partition counts and configs","Use Testcontainers for integration tests to get an isolated Kafka broker per test suite"],interviewQuestions:["How would you set up a local Kafka development environment? What components are needed?","What is KRaft mode and how does it simplify Kafka deployment?","How do you create, list, and describe topics using the Kafka CLI?","How would you verify that a producer successfully sent messages to a topic?"],resources:[{label:"Confluent Docker Quickstart",url:"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/platform-quickstart.html"},{label:"Kafka UI (GitHub)",url:"https://github.com/provectus/kafka-ui"}]},{id:"messaging-patterns",title:"Messaging Patterns",category:"patterns",order:8,explanation:`Distributed systems require well-known messaging patterns to handle failures gracefully, maintain data consistency, and scale reliably. The saga pattern manages distributed transactions by breaking them into a sequence of local transactions, each with a compensating action for rollback. Unlike two-phase commit (2PC), sagas do not lock resources across services, making them suitable for microservice architectures.

The transactional outbox pattern solves the dual-write problem: how to atomically update a database AND publish an event to Kafka. Instead of writing to both directly (which risks one succeeding and the other failing), you write the event to an "outbox" table in the same database transaction as the business data. A separate process (CDC via Debezium or a polling publisher) reads the outbox table and publishes events to Kafka. This guarantees that if the database write succeeds, the event will eventually be published.

Dead letter queues (DLQs) capture messages that cannot be processed after exhausting retry attempts. Instead of losing the message or blocking the queue, failed messages are routed to a DLQ for manual inspection and reprocessing. Retry with exponential backoff adds increasing delays between retries (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s) with jitter to prevent thundering herd when a downstream service recovers. Competing consumers distribute work across multiple consumers reading from the same queue, enabling horizontal scaling of message processing.`,useCases:[{title:"Transactional Outbox for Order Events",scenario:"An order service must save the order to its database AND publish an OrderPlaced event to Kafka. If the DB write succeeds but the Kafka publish fails, downstream services never learn about the order. If Kafka succeeds but the DB fails, an event exists for a non-existent order.",implementation:`In the same database transaction that inserts the order, also insert a row into the outbox table: INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_type, aggregate_id, event_type, payload) VALUES ('Order', 123, 'OrderPlaced', '{"orderId": 123, ...}'). Debezium monitors the outbox table via CDC and publishes each new row to the corresponding Kafka topic. After publication, the outbox row can be cleaned up.`},{title:"Retry with Dead Letter Queue",scenario:"A notification service consumes events from Kafka to send emails. The email provider occasionally returns 500 errors. Failed messages should be retried with backoff, and permanently failed messages should be captured for investigation.",implementation:"Configure retry topics: notifications-retry-1 (1 min delay), notifications-retry-2 (5 min delay), notifications-retry-3 (30 min delay). On failure, republish to the next retry topic with incremented retry count in headers. After exhausting all retry topics, publish to notifications-dlq. A separate DLQ consumer dashboard lets operators inspect failed messages, fix the issue, and replay them."},{title:"Saga: Multi-Service Booking",scenario:"A travel booking must coordinate flight, hotel, and car rental services. Each service has its own database. If hotel booking fails after flight is confirmed, the flight must be cancelled.",implementation:"Orchestrator saga: BookingSaga service publishes BookFlight command. On FlightBooked event, publishes BookHotel. On HotelBookingFailed, publishes CancelFlight (compensating action). Saga state is persisted in a saga_instances table with status tracking. Each step is idempotent and has a corresponding compensation. Timeouts trigger compensation if a step does not respond."}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-topics.sh --create --topic orders-dlq --partitions 1 --replication-factor 3 --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Create a dead letter queue topic for failed order processing messages"},{command:"kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic orders-dlq --from-beginning --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --property print.headers=true",description:"Inspect DLQ messages with their headers (error reason, retry count, original topic)"},{command:"kafka-topics.sh --create --topic orders-retry-1 --config retention.ms=300000 --partitions 3 --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Create a retry topic with 5-minute retention for delayed reprocessing"}],bestPractices:["Use the transactional outbox pattern whenever you need to atomically update a database and publish an event","Implement DLQs for every consumer: failed messages should never be silently dropped","Add exponential backoff with jitter to retries to prevent thundering herd on service recovery","Design every saga step to be idempotent and to have a compensating action for rollback","Include failure metadata (error message, retry count, timestamp, original topic) in DLQ message headers","Set alerts on DLQ message count -- a growing DLQ indicates a systematic processing issue"],interviewQuestions:["What is the transactional outbox pattern and what problem does it solve?","How does the saga pattern differ from two-phase commit? What are the trade-offs?","Design a retry strategy for a Kafka consumer. How would you handle poison pill messages?","What is a dead letter queue and when should you use one?","Explain the dual-write problem and two different approaches to solve it."],resources:[{label:"Microservices.io: Transactional Outbox",url:"https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html"},{label:"Confluent: Dead Letter Queue",url:"https://www.confluent.io/blog/kafka-connect-deep-dive-error-handling-dead-letter-queues/"}]},{id:"ordering-guarantees",title:"Ordering & Delivery Guarantees",category:"patterns",order:9,explanation:`Kafka guarantees ordering only within a single partition, not across partitions. If you need all events for a given entity (order, user, account) to be processed in order, you must use a consistent partition key (e.g., order ID). All records with the same key hash to the same partition, and a single consumer reads each partition sequentially. Across partitions, there is no ordering guarantee -- consumer group members process partitions independently.

Exactly-once semantics (EOS) in Kafka is achieved through the combination of idempotent producers, transactions, and consumer offset management. An idempotent producer assigns sequence numbers to each record, and the broker rejects duplicates. Kafka transactions allow a producer to atomically write records to multiple topic-partitions and commit consumer offsets in a single operation, implementing the read-process-write pattern without duplicates.

Idempotent consumers are equally important and are your responsibility as the application developer. Even with Kafka EOS, your downstream systems (databases, APIs) may see duplicates due to consumer restarts. Design your consumers to handle duplicates gracefully: use upserts instead of inserts, track processed event IDs in a deduplication table, or use natural idempotency (setting a value is idempotent, incrementing a counter is not). The combination of Kafka EOS and idempotent consumers provides true end-to-end exactly-once processing.`,useCases:[{title:"Ordered Event Processing for Bank Accounts",scenario:"A banking system processes deposit and withdrawal events. Processing a withdrawal before its preceding deposit could incorrectly overdraft the account. Events must be processed in the exact order they occurred for each account.",implementation:"Use account_id as the Kafka message key. All events for account #12345 go to the same partition and are consumed by the same consumer in order. Set max.in.flight.requests.per.connection=1 (or use idempotent producer which handles this automatically) to prevent out-of-order delivery from retries. Use manual offset commit after persisting the transaction to the database."},{title:"Exactly-Once Stream Processing",scenario:"A Kafka Streams application reads purchase events, aggregates daily spending per user, and writes results to an output topic. The application must not double-count purchases if it restarts mid-batch.",implementation:"Set processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2 in the Kafka Streams config. This enables transactional producers and consumer offset commits within the same transaction. The Streams app atomically reads input, updates the local state store, writes output, and commits offsets. On failure, the transaction is aborted and processing resumes from the last committed offset with no duplicates."}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-console-producer.sh --topic accounts --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --property parse.key=true --property key.separator=: --producer-property enable.idempotence=true",description:"Produce idempotent keyed messages for testing ordering guarantees"},{command:"kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic accounts --partition 0 --offset earliest --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Consume from a specific partition to verify message ordering"},{command:"kafka-transactions.sh --describe --transactional-id my-txn-id --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Describe transaction state for debugging exactly-once processing issues"}],bestPractices:["Use a consistent partition key (entity ID) when ordering matters; never rely on cross-partition ordering","Enable idempotent producers (default in Kafka 3.0+) to prevent duplicate messages from network retries","For exactly-once processing, use Kafka transactions with processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2 in Streams","Design consumers to be idempotent regardless of Kafka delivery guarantees -- defense in depth","Avoid changing partition count on topics that require ordering: repartitioning changes the key-to-partition mapping"],interviewQuestions:["How does Kafka guarantee message ordering? Under what conditions can ordering be violated?","Explain exactly-once semantics in Kafka. How do idempotent producers, transactions, and consumers work together?","What happens to ordering guarantees when you increase the partition count of an existing topic?","How would you implement an idempotent consumer for a payment processing system?","What is the difference between exactly-once within Kafka and end-to-end exactly-once with external systems?"],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Exactly-Once Semantics",url:"https://www.confluent.io/blog/exactly-once-semantics-are-possible-heres-how-apache-kafka-does-it/"},{label:"Kafka Transactions (KIP-98)",url:"https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-98+-+Exactly+Once+Delivery+and+Transactional+Messaging"}]},{id:"kafka-production",title:"Kafka in Production",category:"production",order:10,explanation:`Running Kafka in production requires careful attention to monitoring, capacity planning, and operational procedures. The most critical metric is consumer lag: the difference between the latest offset (log-end offset) and the consumer's committed offset. Growing lag means consumers are falling behind producers, which can lead to data processing delays, stale data in downstream systems, and eventually data loss if retention expires before consumption.

Capacity planning starts with understanding your throughput requirements. Each partition can handle roughly 10 MB/s writes on standard SSDs. A topic with 12 partitions can sustain ~120 MB/s aggregate write throughput. Disk capacity must account for retention (7 days default), replication factor (3x storage), and growth. Network bandwidth between brokers (replication traffic) is often the first bottleneck in a growing cluster. Plan for 60-70% utilization to handle traffic spikes.

Data retention policies control how long Kafka stores messages. Time-based retention (retention.ms, default 7 days) deletes segments older than the threshold. Size-based retention (retention.bytes) caps total storage per partition. Compacted topics (cleanup.policy=compact) retain only the latest value per key, useful for materialized views and configuration topics. In production, use tiered storage (Kafka 3.6+) to move older data to object storage (S3) for cost-effective long retention without growing broker disk.`,useCases:[{title:"Consumer Lag Alerting",scenario:"A critical order processing consumer slows down during Black Friday traffic. The operations team needs to detect lag early and scale consumers before orders pile up and SLAs are breached.",implementation:"Deploy Burrow or use kafka-consumer-groups.sh in a cron job to track lag per partition per consumer group. Set a warning alert at 10K messages lag and a critical alert at 100K. Create a Grafana dashboard showing lag trend, consumer throughput, and partition assignment. On alert: check consumer pod CPU/memory, check downstream service latency, add more consumer instances. For Kubernetes, configure HPA based on consumer lag custom metrics."},{title:"Partition Rebalancing After Broker Addition",scenario:"Your 3-broker cluster is at 80% disk capacity. You add 2 new brokers, but existing partitions remain on the original 3 brokers. The new brokers are idle.",implementation:"Generate a reassignment plan using kafka-reassign-partitions.sh --generate, specifying the new broker IDs. Review the plan to ensure balanced distribution. Execute with --execute and monitor progress with --verify. Throttle replication traffic with --throttle 50000000 (50 MB/s) to avoid impacting production traffic. After completion, verify ISR is fully caught up on all partitions."}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group my-app --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Show consumer lag, partition assignment, and committed offsets for a consumer group"},{command:"kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-replicated-partitions --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"List partitions where replicas have fallen behind the leader (indicates broker issues)"},{command:"kafka-configs.sh --alter --topic orders --add-config retention.ms=604800000 --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Set retention to 7 days for the orders topic"},{command:"kafka-log-dirs.sh --describe --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --topic-list orders",description:"Show disk usage for the orders topic across all brokers"},{command:"kafka-delete-records.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --offset-json-file offsets.json",description:"Delete records before a specific offset to free disk space (emergency operation)"}],bestPractices:["Monitor consumer lag as the primary health metric; alert on both absolute lag and lag growth rate","Set min.insync.replicas=2 with replication.factor=3 to tolerate one broker failure without data loss","Capacity plan at 60-70% utilization to handle traffic spikes and broker failures","Use rack-aware replication to distribute replicas across availability zones","Enable tiered storage for topics with long retention (>7 days) to reduce broker disk costs","Automate partition reassignment after adding brokers; never leave new brokers idle"],interviewQuestions:["How do you monitor Kafka in production? What are the top 5 metrics you would track?","A consumer group shows increasing lag. Walk through your troubleshooting steps.","How would you plan capacity for a Kafka cluster handling 1 million events per second?","What is the impact of increasing replication factor from 2 to 3? What about from 3 to 5?","How do you handle data retention? When would you use compaction vs time-based retention?"],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Monitoring Kafka",url:"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/kafka/monitoring.html"},{label:"LinkedIn: Running Kafka at Scale",url:"https://engineering.linkedin.com/kafka/running-kafka-scale"}]},{id:"kafka-security",title:"Kafka Security & Multi-Tenancy",category:"production",order:11,explanation:`Kafka security covers three pillars: authentication (who is connecting), authorization (what they can do), and encryption (protecting data in transit). Authentication is handled via SASL mechanisms: SASL/PLAIN (username/password, simple but requires TLS), SASL/SCRAM (salted challenge-response, more secure), SASL/GSSAPI (Kerberos, enterprise standard), or SASL/OAUTHBEARER (OAuth 2.0 tokens, modern cloud-native). Mutual TLS (mTLS) provides both encryption and client certificate-based authentication.

Authorization uses Access Control Lists (ACLs) to define which principals (users/service accounts) can perform which operations (READ, WRITE, CREATE, DELETE) on which resources (topics, consumer groups, cluster). For example, you can allow the "order-service" principal to WRITE to "orders" topic and READ from "payments" topic, while denying access to all other topics. Confluent Platform extends this with Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) for more manageable policies.

Multi-tenancy on a shared Kafka cluster requires isolation at multiple levels. Topic naming conventions (tenant-a.orders, tenant-b.orders) provide logical separation. Quotas (produce_byte_rate, fetch_byte_rate, request_percentage) prevent one tenant from monopolizing cluster resources. ACLs restrict each tenant to their own topics. For stronger isolation, use separate listener ports or even separate clusters per tenant. In practice, most organizations use a shared cluster with topic prefixes, ACLs, and quotas for cost efficiency.`,useCases:[{title:"Securing Microservice Communication",scenario:"Multiple microservice teams share a Kafka cluster. Each team should only access their own topics. A compromised service should not be able to read data from other teams or delete topics.",implementation:"Enable SASL/SCRAM authentication with unique credentials per service. Create ACLs: kafka-acls.sh --add --allow-principal User:order-service --operation Write --topic orders --bootstrap-server localhost:9092. Deny all by default (allow.everyone.if.no.acl.found=false). Enable TLS for encryption in transit. Each service authenticates with its own SCRAM credentials, and ACLs restrict access to only the topics it needs."},{title:"Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform",scenario:"A SaaS platform hosts 50 tenants on a shared Kafka cluster. Each tenant generates different volumes of data. One large tenant should not be able to degrade performance for others.",implementation:"Use topic naming convention: {tenant_id}.{domain}.{event_type} (e.g., tenant-42.orders.created). Create ACLs per tenant using topic prefix: kafka-acls.sh --add --allow-principal User:tenant-42 --operation All --topic tenant-42. --resource-pattern-type prefixed. Set per-client quotas: kafka-configs.sh --alter --add-config producer_byte_rate=10485760,consumer_byte_rate=20971520 --entity-type users --entity-name tenant-42. Monitor per-tenant throughput and lag."}],keyCommands:[{command:"kafka-acls.sh --list --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"List all ACLs configured on the cluster"},{command:"kafka-acls.sh --add --allow-principal User:order-svc --operation Write --topic orders --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Grant write permission on the orders topic to the order-svc principal"},{command:"kafka-acls.sh --add --deny-principal User:analytics --operation Write --topic orders --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Explicitly deny write access for analytics service on orders topic"},{command:"kafka-configs.sh --alter --add-config producer_byte_rate=10485760 --entity-type users --entity-name tenant-42 --bootstrap-server localhost:9092",description:"Set a 10 MB/s producer quota for tenant-42 to prevent resource monopolization"},{command:'openssl req -new -x509 -keyout ca-key -out ca-cert -days 365 -subj "/CN=KafkaCA"',description:"Generate a self-signed CA certificate for Kafka TLS setup"}],bestPractices:["Always enable TLS for encryption in transit, even in internal networks (zero-trust security)","Use SASL/SCRAM or mTLS for authentication; avoid SASL/PLAIN without TLS","Set allow.everyone.if.no.acl.found=false to enforce deny-by-default access control","Use topic prefix-based ACLs for multi-tenant setups to simplify management","Configure per-client quotas to prevent noisy neighbor problems on shared clusters"],interviewQuestions:["What authentication mechanisms does Kafka support? When would you use each one?","How do Kafka ACLs work? How would you set up topic-level access control for 10 microservices?","How would you design a multi-tenant Kafka architecture for a SaaS platform?","What is the difference between encryption in transit (TLS) and encryption at rest in Kafka?","How do Kafka quotas work and what problem do they solve?"],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Kafka Security",url:"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/security/index.html"},{label:"Apache Kafka: Security Documentation",url:"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#security"}]},{id:"queue-comparison",title:"Queue Comparison",category:"alternatives",order:12,explanation:`Choosing the right messaging system depends on your requirements for throughput, latency, ordering, delivery guarantees, operational complexity, and ecosystem integration. Apache Kafka excels at high-throughput, durable event streaming with replay capability. It is ideal for event sourcing, CDC, stream processing, and log aggregation. Its persistent, replayable log makes it the default choice when you need to store events long-term or have multiple consumers reading the same data at different speeds.

RabbitMQ is a traditional message broker that excels at complex routing, low-latency task distribution, and the classic producer-consumer pattern. It supports multiple protocols (AMQP, MQTT, STOMP), flexible exchange types (direct, topic, fanout, headers), and built-in features like priority queues, message TTL, and dead letter exchanges. Choose RabbitMQ when you need smart routing, low per-message latency (sub-millisecond), or when your use case is task queuing rather than event streaming.

AWS SQS is a fully managed queue service that requires zero operational overhead. It offers standard queues (at-least-once, best-effort ordering, near-unlimited throughput) and FIFO queues (exactly-once, strict ordering, 300-3000 msg/s). SQS is ideal when you are on AWS, want zero ops, and your throughput fits within FIFO limits. Redis Pub/Sub provides fire-and-forget messaging with microsecond latency but no persistence or replay. Redis Streams adds persistence and consumer groups to Redis. Apache Pulsar combines Kafka-like streaming with RabbitMQ-like queuing, multi-tenancy, and geo-replication built in, but has a smaller ecosystem.`,useCases:[{title:"Choosing Between Kafka and RabbitMQ",scenario:"A team is building a new microservice architecture and needs to decide between Kafka and RabbitMQ for inter-service communication.",implementation:"Choose Kafka if: you need event replay (multiple consumers reading at different speeds), high throughput (100K+ msg/s), event sourcing, or stream processing. Choose RabbitMQ if: you need complex routing (route messages based on headers/patterns), priority queues, low latency per message, or classic request-reply patterns. Many organizations use both: Kafka for the event backbone and RabbitMQ for task queuing within services."},{title:"Serverless Event Processing on AWS",scenario:"A startup on AWS needs asynchronous processing for user uploads (resize images, extract metadata, update search index). The team wants minimal operational overhead and pay-per-use pricing.",implementation:"Use SQS standard queue + Lambda. When a file is uploaded to S3, an S3 event notification sends a message to SQS. Lambda polls SQS and processes each message (resize, extract, index). Failed messages go to a DLQ. No servers to manage, auto-scales to zero. For ordered processing (e.g., user profile updates), use SQS FIFO with message group ID = user ID."},{title:"Real-Time Chat with Redis Pub/Sub",scenario:"A chat application needs to broadcast messages to all connected users in a room with sub-millisecond latency. Message history is stored in a database, so persistence in the message broker is not required.",implementation:"Use Redis Pub/Sub with one channel per chat room. When a user sends a message, publish to the room channel. All connected WebSocket servers subscribe to the channels of rooms they serve. Messages are delivered instantly with ~0.1ms latency. Use Redis Streams instead if you need message history, consumer groups, or acknowledgment."}],keyCommands:[{command:"docker run -d --name rabbitmq -p 5672:5672 -p 15672:15672 rabbitmq:3-management",description:"Start RabbitMQ with management UI for comparison testing"},{command:"aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url <url> --attribute-names ApproximateNumberOfMessages",description:"Check SQS queue depth to compare with Kafka consumer lag"},{command:"redis-cli SUBSCRIBE chat:room:42",description:"Subscribe to a Redis Pub/Sub channel (fire-and-forget messaging)"},{command:'redis-cli XADD chat:room:42 * user alice msg "hello"',description:"Add a message to a Redis Stream (persistent, replayable alternative to Pub/Sub)"},{command:"docker run -d --name pulsar -p 6650:6650 -p 8080:8080 apachepulsar/pulsar:latest bin/pulsar standalone",description:"Start Apache Pulsar standalone for comparison testing"}],bestPractices:["Default to Kafka for event streaming (replay, multiple consumers, high throughput) and RabbitMQ for task queuing (routing, priorities, low latency)","Use SQS when you want zero operational overhead on AWS and your throughput fits within limits","Use Redis Pub/Sub only for ephemeral, real-time messaging where message loss is acceptable","Consider Apache Pulsar if you need built-in multi-tenancy, geo-replication, and unified streaming + queuing","Do not choose a technology based on peak benchmarks; evaluate operational complexity, ecosystem maturity, and team expertise"],interviewQuestions:["Compare Kafka, RabbitMQ, and SQS. When would you choose each one?","What is the fundamental architectural difference between Kafka (log-based) and RabbitMQ (queue-based)?","How does SQS FIFO differ from Kafka in terms of ordering guarantees and throughput?","When would you use Redis Streams instead of Kafka?","Your company uses Kafka but a team wants to use RabbitMQ for a new service. How would you evaluate this decision?"],resources:[{label:"Confluent: Kafka vs RabbitMQ",url:"https://www.confluent.io/kafka-vs-rabbitmq/"},{label:"AWS: SQS vs SNS vs EventBridge",url:"https://aws.amazon.com/messaging/"}]}];export{e as a};
